11. PHASE NINE: SECURITY AND SESSIONS

Build a dedicated security page using the project's existing authentication infrastructure.

A. Password management

If password authentication is supported, allow the customer to change their password through a secure workflow.

Requirements:

- Verify the current password where appropriate.
- Validate the new password on the server.
- Use the authentication system's secure password-hashing implementation.
- Never return password hashes to the frontend.
- Never log plaintext passwords.
- Never store passwords in local storage or session storage.
- Avoid revealing whether an arbitrary email belongs to an account.

If the project uses an external authentication provider, follow its supported password-management flow instead of implementing a competing password system.

B. Active sessions

If supported by the authentication provider, allow customers to inspect and revoke their active sessions.

Do not claim to provide session management if the provider does not expose the necessary functionality.

A session-revocation action must invalidate the relevant server-side session or token according to the application's architecture.

C. Account deletion

Provide a clearly labeled account deletion or deactivation workflow only after implementing the relevant backend and business rules.

Requirements:

- Require appropriate reauthentication or confirmation.
- Explain the consequences before final confirmation.
- Protect against accidental submission.
- Preserve records that must legally or operationally remain, where applicable.
- Avoid destroying order history or accounting records that must be retained.
- Handle personal data according to applicable retention and privacy requirements.
- Revoke active sessions as appropriate.
- Avoid leaving orphaned records or broken relationships.

Do not permanently delete an account immediately when the user merely opens the deletion dialog.

Do not claim that account data has been deleted until the backend operation has succeeded.

D. Authentication and authorization

Enforce access control server-side.

Use the project's established session or token verification process.

Do not trust:

- User IDs supplied in request bodies.
- Roles supplied by the browser.
- Client-side route guards alone.
- Local storage as proof of authentication.
- Hidden form fields as authorization controls.

Implement appropriate protections against unauthorized access, CSRF where applicable, session misuse, and mass assignment.

Use secure cookie settings when cookie-based sessions are used.

Follow the existing authentication provider's recommended security practices.

